1️⃣ “If SameSite=Lax is enabled, is CSRF fully solved?”
No. SameSite=Lax reduces CSRF risk, but does not eliminate it.
Let’s reason carefully.
When a cookie has:
Set-Cookie: session=abc; SameSite=Lax
The browser behavior becomes:
• Cookies ARE sent on top-level navigations (GET requests)
• Cookies are NOT sent on cross-site POST via forms (usually)
So if your sensitive endpoint is:
POST /transfer-money
And uses POST only → Lax protects it from simple form-based CSRF.
But here’s the bypass:
If your sensitive endpoint is triggered via:
GET /delete-account?id=123
Then an attacker can embed:
<img src="https://bank.com/delete-account?id=123">
Top-level GET → cookie sent → CSRF succeeds.
Even worse:
If your app uses:
	• GET for state change
	• Or supports GET fallback
You are vulnerable.
Sticky memory model:
	Lax protects cross-site POST.
	Lax does NOT protect top-level GET.
	State-changing GET = disaster.

2️⃣ “Why doesn’t CORS protect against CSRF?”
Let’s clarify browser behavior.
CORS controls:
	Whether attacker JS can read the response.
CSRF depends on:
	Whether the browser sends the request.
Example:
Attacker page:
<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
   <input name="amount" value="1000">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()</script>
Browser:
• Sends POST
• Includes victim cookies
Even if CORS blocks reading response, the state change already happened.
So:
CORS ≠ CSRF protection
CORS protects response confidentiality
CSRF exploits automatic request sending
Sticky memory:
	CSRF is about sending.
	CORS is about reading.

3️⃣ “How does CSRF work if the site uses JWT instead of cookies?”
CSRF requires:
	Automatic credential inclusion.
Cookies are automatically sent by browser.
JWT stored in:
• localStorage → NOT auto-sent
• memory → NOT auto-sent
• Authorization header → only sent by JS
So:
If JWT is stored in localStorage and attached manually:
fetch('/transfer', {
  headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer ' + token }
})
Attacker page cannot read token → cannot attach header → CSRF fails.
BUT:
If JWT is stored in:
Set-Cookie: access_token=xyz;
Then CSRF is possible.
Sticky mental model:
	CSRF requires automatic credential inclusion.
No automatic inclusion → no CSRF.

4️⃣ “Is CSRF possible in a REST API?”
Yes — if the API uses cookies.
Example:
Single Page App talking to backend.
If authentication is cookie-based:
fetch('/api/update', { method: 'POST' })
Attacker page can submit form to that endpoint.
Even though it’s “API”, it’s still browser + cookie.
REST ≠ CSRF-safe.

Only:
• Token in header
• No cookies
reduces CSRF risk

5️⃣ “Why are custom headers effective CSRF protection?”

This tests deep browser understanding.

Attacker cannot send arbitrary custom headers cross-site without triggering CORS preflight.

Example:

Backend requires:

X-CSRF-Token: abc123

Browser behavior:
Cross-site JS sending custom header triggers OPTIONS preflight.

Preflight will fail if not allowed.

Therefore attacker cannot send the header.

Key point:

CSRF protection works because attacker cannot forge custom headers cross-site.

Sticky memory:

Simple form → no custom headers
Custom header → requires CORS preflight
No preflight allowed → attack blocked

6️⃣ “Double-submit cookie pattern — explain why it works.”
Flow:
Step 1 — What Double Submit Cookie Actually Does
Server sets:
Set-Cookie: csrf_token=RANDOM123; Secure; SameSite=Lax

Now victim’s browser stores:
csrf_token = RANDOM123

When user submits a form legitimately, frontend JS reads cookie and sends:
POST /transfer
Cookie: csrf_token=RANDOM123; session=ABC
X-CSRF-Token: RANDOM123

Server checks:
cookie.csrf_token == header.csrf_token

If equal → valid request.

Step 2 — What Attacker Can Do Cross-Site
Attacker controls evil.com.
They host:
<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
   <input name="amount" value="1000">
</form>
<script>
  document.forms[0].submit();
</script>

When victim visits evil.com:
Browser automatically attaches cookies for bank.com:
Cookie: csrf_token=RANDOM123; session=ABC
So attacker can cause the cookie to be sent.

Step 4 — Why can’t attacker just send the same value in header and cookie without reading it?
Can attacker set the Cookie header manually?
No.
Browsers do NOT allow JavaScript to set arbitrary Cookie headers for another domain.
If attacker tries:
fetch("https://bank.com/transfer", {
  headers: {
    "Cookie": "csrf_token=FAKE"
  }
})
Browser ignores that header.
The browser controls the Cookie header, not JavaScript.
So attacker cannot:
	• Modify
	• Replace
	• Duplicate
	• Inject a custom cookie header
The browser automatically attaches the real cookie.
Attacker cannot override it.

Step 5 — The Only Way It Breaks
Double-submit fails only if:
-Token is predictable (e.g., user ID)
-Token is not random
-There is XSS (attacker can read cookie)
-Cookie is not SameSite and attacker finds side channel
-Cookie is accessible cross-site via misconfiguration

Step 6 — Critical Browser Fact
JavaScript cannot:
-Set arbitrary Cookie headers
-Read cookies of another origin
-Read response of cross-site request without CORS
This is enforced by:
-Same-Origin Policy
-Browser network stack rules

This is the real reason double-submit works.

Why attacker fails:
It works because the browser automatically includes cookies cross-site, but the Same-Origin Policy prevents attacker-controlled JavaScript from reading those cookies. 
Since the attacker cannot know the csrf_token value, they cannot replicate it in the request body or header. 
The server verifies equality between cookie and header, and mismatch causes rejection.

Sticky memory:
Browser holds the cookie in a locked vault.
Attacker can send a request that includes the vault contents.
But attacker cannot open the vault to see what’s inside.
Double-submit requires knowing what’s inside.
Since attacker cannot see inside, they fail.

7️⃣ “Can CSRF happen with JSON APIs?”
Yes — if server accepts:
Content-Type: application/json
But here’s nuance:
A simple HTML form cannot send JSON with proper header.
It sends:
application/x-www-form-urlencoded
If backend only accepts JSON:
Content-Type: application/json
Then CSRF via form fails.
But:
If backend also accepts urlencoded fallback → vulnerability.

Restrict content types strictly.

8️⃣ LOGIN CSRF
Most people think CSRF only means:
	“Force a victim to transfer money.”

Step 1 — Normal Authentication Flow
User goes to:
POST /login
username=dipika
password=correct_password
Server verifies and sets:
Set-Cookie: session=XYZ123
Now browser holds session for Dipika.

Step 2 — What Is Login CSRF?
In login CSRF, attacker authenticates the victim into the attacker's account.
Let’s walk the attack step-by-step.
Step A — Attacker Prepares Their Own Account
Attacker logs into bank normally as:
username = attacker
password = pass123
Attacker knows these credentials.

Step B — Attacker Crafts a Malicious Link or Form
<form action="https://bank.com/login" method="POST">
  <input name="username" value="attacker">
  <input name="password" value="pass123">
</form>
<script>
  document.forms[0].submit();
</script>
Or:
https://bank.com/login?username=attacker&password=pass123

Step C — Victim Visits Attacker Site
Victim is not logged in currently.
Victim visits attacker.com.
Form auto-submits.
Browser sends request to bank.com:
POST /login
username=attacker
password=pass123
Bank validates.
Bank sets:
Set-Cookie: session=ATTACKER_SESSION
Browser now stores session for attacker.

Step 3 — What Just Happened?
Victim is now authenticated as attacker.
Victim thinks:
	“I’m browsing normally.”
Victim might:
• Upload personal documents
• Add their credit card
• Perform transactions
But all actions happen inside attacker’s account.
Attacker later logs into their account and sees:
• Victim’s uploaded data
• Victim’s card saved
• Victim’s personal info
No money transfer happened during attack.
The attack was:
	Authentication state manipulation.

Why This Works
Because login endpoints often do NOT require CSRF tokens.
Developers think:
	“Login is safe. It’s just authentication.”
But login changes session state. And session state is security-sensitive.

CSRF is not only about state-changing actions like transfer/delete.
It is about:
	Any request that changes security context. Authentication is security context.

Modern Mitigation
Login endpoints must also include CSRF protection.
Or:
• Use SameSite cookies
• Require CSRF token
• Avoid GET login endpoints

Sticky Memory Model
Normal CSRF:
	Victim acts in their account without consent.
Login CSRF:
	Victim acts in attacker’s account without knowing.
Authentication state is part of application state.

9️⃣ “How does SameSite=None + Secure behave?”
If cookie is:
SameSite=None; Secure
Then browser will send cookie cross-site.
This is required for third-party SSO. But it reintroduces CSRF risk.
So systems relying on cross-site cookies must use CSRF tokens.









